#[
  #set text(size: 9.0pt)
  #set par(leading: 0.63em, spacing: 0.68em)

  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Whether to leave and what leaving costs. The school, the board and the family are all parts of that one question.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A claim with evidence behind it. *Education* is a subject; *integrity in education is expensive* is a theme.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The parallel — a people once taken and now leaving willingly — is the hinge of the novel's argument about japa.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Lekki, Lagos, and Stardom Schools inside it. Ibadan holds one chapter's errand, Abuja is never used, and London is never shown.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    London: Seri's city is a voice on the telephone, a set of figures and a dream. The other three are scenes.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The book makes the case for leaving and then shows the bill. The two absolutes are the options it spends four chapters refusing.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    £10,000 against ₦400,000 is the strongest argument in the book — and the novel gives it its full weight before answering it.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Unaccountable ownership: the name stays wrong, the rite is performed, and the school survives.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It grew to more than seventy pupils and then failed when the roads went bad and the fee-paying families left — a failure of circumstance, not of principle.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Nothing has been stolen. The board's alarm is at a staff that might one day leave and compete — the snake in its own roof.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The novel's deepest suspicion is of success that costs nothing and asks no work — the same temptation that runs through Beesway.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Bepo's aim is that they should see Nigeria before they think of leaving it — the waterfalls, the festivals and Badagry are all part of that.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Education, talent and determination can move a person anywhere — and some of the wealthy of Banana Island began in exactly those streets.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The corruption is real and so is the answerability of Stardom, the working excursions and the last chapter's title.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Correction is the difference the two schools exist to show. The rest are measurable things the novel never treats as causes.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The chairman's proverb is precise: a staff with money of its own may take the school's ideas and set up against it.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The cow at Beesway and the ₦35,000 rite at Fruitful Future are the same offer: results without the labour.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A family sells its furniture, closes its business, learns new trades — and the visa, when it comes, destroys the family instead of moving it.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Forms at ₦50,000 for head boy and ₦40,000 for deputies, a walk-out, and two families in court: the school is a small edition of the country.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The parent withdraws his child, the school loses a fee-payer, and the refusal is not rewarded in this world with a school that survives.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Queues, network failures, agents and a ₦100,000 payment to move things along: Chapter 10 is the novel's least romantic patriotism.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The excursions take the pupils from Banana Island to Ajegunle in the same chapter, and the school sits between the two.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The university is never a scene. The other three are places the novel takes the reader to.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The structure is forensic: the strongest case is presented first, then tested against Badagry, the family and the mission.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The dream is the last turn of the screw; the return follows it on Monday morning.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Not the money. The past is put physically in front of him at Badagry, and the unfinished work waits at the school gate.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The novel shows authority as a weight carried in front of an assembly, not as a reward.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The Yoruba word for escape has become the Nigerian name for leaving the country — and the novel uses the street word rather than a report's vocabulary.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    He walks back in on Monday and says he has come to complete his mission; the pupils drown his voice with the school's victory song.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The paper wants a claim the book supports. A subject can be true of any novel and proves nothing.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Characters may believe it; the novel never endorses it. The other three are claims the book argues and illustrates.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    When the carver leaves, the carvings remain — Bepo's students are the carvings, and the proverb is the chapter's whole argument.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The weeping is the price of a choice already made — which is why it comes before the reader has been told what the choice is.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Bribes in envelopes are almost absent. The novel's corruption is a parent's certainty, an owner's vanity, a board's fear and a queue that must be paid to move.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It has a board that argues and an MD who can be corrected — the one thing Beesway never has.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The chapter travels from the natural wonders to the cultural sites to the slave trade — and ends with the analogy that the rest of the book turns on.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A visa, a DNA result and a family ordered out of its own house: migration and the family are the whole of the chapter.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The title joins the place to the office: Lekki's headmaster, in a school that has to look successful in a wealthy suburb.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The cap on loans is a protective reflex, not a punishment: the board's first instinct is the survival of the family business.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Bepo, standing at the Point of No Return and later dreaming of the ship. The novel never puts the comparison in another character's mouth.
  ]

]

